.TH XSTART 1 "20 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
xstart \- start X on this machine's display
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xstart
[
.B \-n
] [
.B \-xdm
|
.B \-dtlogin
] [
.BI : N
] [
.IR W x H
] [
.IB key = value
\&... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xstart
starts an X session on the display card, mode and depth that
.B /etc/display
names (see
.BR display (4)),
with the X server and arguments the card's file in
.B /etc/display.d
gives (see
.BR display.d (4)).
An argument changes one field of
.B /etc/display
for this session only:
.IR W x H
is the mode, and
.BR card= ,
.BR mode= ,
.BR depth= ,
.BR session= ,
.B monsw=
and
.B once=
the others.
.BI : N
is the display number (default
.BR :0 ).
.PP
In order,
.B xstart
finds the card (with
.BR "card auto" ,
the first that is there, the chipset display last: its probe command, when
its file has one, and then its device node, say so; see
.BR display.d (4)),
checks the mode
and depth against the card's, raises the limits the card asks for (a limit
already as high, or unlimited, is left as it is, never lowered), switches
the monitor to the card when the card shares it, and runs the server through
.B xinit
(X11R6's, else the system's) with the session:
.BR ~/.xsession ,
else
.BR ~/.xinitrc ,
else the
.B session
of
.BR /etc/display ,
else
.BR /usr/lib/amix/xsession .
The server runs through a small script
.B xstart
writes in
.B /tmp
and removes afterwards: it gives the card's environment
.RB ( env )
to the server alone, notes the server's process id and starts the server
in its own place, so that id is the server's.
The session runs through a second such script.
When the session ends while the server still runs (a normal end), it sends
the server TERM while
.B xinit
still holds its connection, so the server ends at once and gives the
display back itself, instead of resetting for another session first (a
reset can take half a minute on a 68LC060, and
.B xinit
kills a server that is still resetting after 10 seconds, with no hand-back).
A client that comes and goes, before the session or during it, does not end
the server.
.B xinit
then says
.RB \(lq "connection to X server lost" \(rq:
the server it was about to stop has already ended, and that line belongs
to a normal end.
When the session ends, the monitor is switched back.
When it did not end that way, the card's
.B restore
command runs too, and
.B xstart
says why in one line: the server ended on its own (it crashed or was
killed, and
.B xinit
ended with status 0), or
.B xinit
ended the session itself (it caught a signal and stopped the server, and
ended with status 1, or it failed), with its status.
A
.B restore
command that is not there is said once, at the start, and never run.
.PP
With
.B once yes
in
.B /etc/display
or
.B once=yes
on the command line, the server is also given the card's
.B once
option
.RB ( display.d (4)),
so that it ends whenever its last client leaves.
That is off by default: a client that connects and leaves before the
session's first one (a probe, an
.BR xdpyinfo )
then ends the server, and the session never starts.
.PP
When
.B xinit
ends while the server still runs (it was killed, and so did not stop the
server), the display is still the server's.
.B xstart
says so in one line and ends the server: TERM, then KILL when it is still
there after
.B XSTART_KILLWAIT
seconds (10 when not set).
Before each signal it checks with
.B "ps \-ef"
that the process with the noted id still runs the card's server on this
display, so another process that has the id by now is never signalled.
Only when the server is gone does the card's
.B restore
command run and the monitor switch back.
When the server cannot be ended,
.B xstart
says so, hands nothing back and exits 1.
.PP
When an X server for the display is still running (one still ending, or
another session's),
.B xstart
says so once and waits for it to end, up to
.B XSTART_WAIT
seconds (60 when not set), looking every 2 seconds; when it is still there,
.B xstart
starts nothing and exits 1.
.B \-xdm
and
.B \-dtlogin
wait the same way.
.PP
The system's
.B startx
is still there and unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
say what would be run, and every mistake in
.B /etc/display
and the card files, and run nothing; an X server still running on the
display is reported.
A card's probe command runs, as it would for a session.
A key
.B xstart
does not know (one from a newer base package) is reported as ignored.
The card's limits are shown with what each would do (kept, or raised
from the value in force), and whether
.B once
is asked for.
A card whose
.B restore
command is not there is warned about, and so is a card with no
.B restore
line whose server is not the system's own: after such a server is killed
or crashes, the console may take no typing until a screen is chosen again.
Neither is a mistake.
.TP
.B \-xdm \fR|\fP \-dtlogin
instead of a session, start X11R6's
.B xdm
or CDE's
.B dtlogin
with the same server line, given as
.B \-server
.RI \(dq: N
.B local
.IR server-line \(dq
to
.BR xdm ,
and in CDE's extended form
.B \-server
.RI \(dq: N
.B local_uid root
.IR server-line \(dq
to
.BR dtlogin ,
which otherwise runs the server as
.B nobody
(and so it cannot open the card's device node); the boot step
.B S95display
does this when
.B /etc/display
says
.B start xdm
or
.BR "start dtlogin" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the session ran (with
.BR \-n :
when neither
.B /etc/display
nor the card used holds a mistake; a key it does not know and a mistake in
another card's file do not count); 1 when the display cannot be set up (no
card answers, a mode or depth the card does not have, no server, no
.BR xinit ,
an X server for the display that did not end in time, a session's server
that could not be ended);
2 on a usage mistake.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/display
the machine's choice.
.TP
.B /etc/display.d/*
the cards.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/amix/xsession
the default session: the user's
.BR ~/.Xresources ,
an
.B xterm
at the top left (with a place of its own, so that
.B twm
does not hold the server waiting for one), and the first window manager
found.
It belongs to the package, which replaces it at each update; a user's own
session is
.BR ~/.xsession .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR display (4),
.BR display.d (4)
.SH NOTES
An X11R6.3 server leaves its socket in
.B /tmp/.X11-unix
when it ends, however it ends; the next server replaces it, so it needs no
removing by hand.
.PP
The keyboard map under X is not yet derived from the console's
.BR /etc/default.kmap ;
a
.B ~/.Xmodmap
run from the session sets it.
